  "grammarNotes": [
    {
      "id": "kleidung-articles",
      "title": "Clothes: der, die, das",
      "body": "Learn every piece of clothing with its article and plural.\n\nTwo things are different from English:\n\n- **die Hose** is singular: it's *one* pair of trousers. *Die Hose **ist** neu.* = The trousers are new. *die Hosen* means several pairs.\n- Shoes come in pairs, so you'll mostly use the plural **die Schuhe**.\n\n**die Kleidung** (clothes) is singular too: *Die Kleidung ist schön.*",
      "table": [
        ["Singular", "Plural", "English"],
        ["die Hose", "die Hosen", "trousers"],
        ["die Jacke", "die Jacken", "jacket"],
        ["das Hemd", "die Hemden", "shirt"],
        ["das Kleid", "die Kleider", "dress"],
        ["das T-Shirt", "die T-Shirts", "T-shirt"],
        ["der Pullover", "die Pullover", "jumper"],
        ["der Rock", "die Röcke", "skirt"],
        ["der Mantel", "die Mäntel", "coat"],
        ["der Schuh", "die Schuhe", "shoe"]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "kleidung-colours",
      "title": "Colours after sein",
      "body": "After **ist** or **sind**, a colour never changes. It stays the same for *der*, *die* and *das* words and for the plural:\n\n- *Der Rock ist **rot**.*\n- *Die Schuhe sind **rot**.*\n\nTo ask about a colour, say **Welche Farbe hat …?** (literally *Which colour has …?*).\n\nBefore a noun, colours take endings (*eine blaue Jacke*). That comes in a later unit; for now, put the colour after *ist* or *sind*.",
      "table": [
        ["German", "English"],
        ["rot", "red"],
        ["blau", "blue"],
        ["grün", "green"],
        ["gelb", "yellow"],
        ["schwarz", "black"],
        ["weiß", "white"],
        ["grau", "grey"],
        ["braun", "brown"]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "kleidung-prices",
      "title": "Prices: kostet or kosten",
      "body": "Ask **Was kostet …?** or **Wie viel kostet …?** Both mean *How much is …?*\n\nThe verb matches the thing: one thing **kostet**, several things **kosten**.\n\n- *Die Jacke **kostet** neunundvierzig Euro.*\n- *Die Schuhe **kosten** achtzig Euro.*\n\nGermans write a comma where English uses a dot. Say the euros, then the cents, and leave out the word *Cent*. *Euro* and *Cent* don't take a plural after a number.\n\nToo expensive? **Das ist zu teuer!**",
      "table": [
        ["Written", "Said"],
        ["5,00 €", "fünf Euro"],
        ["12,50 €", "zwölf Euro fünfzig"],
        ["49,99 €", "neunundvierzig Euro neunundneunzig"],
        ["0,80 €", "achtzig Cent"]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "kleidung-dieser-welcher",
      "title": "dieser and welcher",
      "body": "**dieser** (this) and **welcher** (which) follow the pattern of *der, die, das*. The ending copies the end of the article:\n\n- de**r** → dies**er**, welch**er**\n- di**e** → dies**e**, welch**e**\n- da**s** → dies**es**, welch**es**\n\nWhen a *der* word is the **object**, *der* becomes *den*, and these words change the same way: **diesen**, **welchen**. *die*, *das* and plural words don't change.\n\n- *Ich kaufe **diesen** Mantel.*\n- ***Welchen** Rock möchtest du?*",
      "table": [
        ["", "der word", "die word", "das word", "plural"],
        ["the", "der Rock", "die Hose", "das Hemd", "die Schuhe"],
        ["this / these", "dieser Rock", "diese Hose", "dieses Hemd", "diese Schuhe"],
        ["which", "welcher Rock", "welche Hose", "welches Hemd", "welche Schuhe"],
        ["object: the", "den Rock", "die Hose", "das Hemd", "die Schuhe"],
        ["object: this", "diesen Rock", "diese Hose", "dieses Hemd", "diese Schuhe"],
        ["object: which", "welchen Rock", "welche Hose", "welches Hemd", "welche Schuhe"]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "kleidung-shop-phrases",
      "title": "In a clothes shop",
      "body": "Use **Sie** with shop assistants. **anprobieren** (to try on) is a separable verb: the prefix *an* jumps to the end, *Ich probiere die Jacke **an**.* After *können* or *möchten* it stays in one piece at the end: *Kann ich die Jacke **anprobieren**?*\n\n**Das gefällt mir.** means *I like it*. Learn it as a fixed phrase.\n\nIn **auch in Blau** (also in blue), the colour is used as a name, so it gets a capital letter.",
      "table": [
        ["You want to…", "Say"],
        ["say what you're looking for", "Ich suche eine Jacke."],
        ["give your size", "Größe M. / Größe achtunddreißig."],
        ["ask for another colour", "Haben Sie die Jacke auch in Blau?"],
        ["try something on", "Kann ich die Jacke anprobieren?"],
        ["say you like it", "Das gefällt mir."],
        ["ask the price", "Was kostet die Jacke?"],
        ["say no politely", "Das ist leider zu teuer."],
        ["buy it", "Ich kaufe diese Jacke."]
      ]
    }
  ],
